<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Shayuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/537">537</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Shayuan was fought in 537 between the Eastern Wei under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, near the Wei River in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Though heavily outnumbered, Yuwen Tai ambushed Gao Huan's army among reed marshes and routed it, capturing tens of thousands of men. The victory secured the survival of the Western Wei, forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-shayuan.jpg" alt="Battle of Shayuan" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_and_Southern_Dynasties_3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Northern Wei, rulers of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for over a century, were shaken from 523 by the Six Garrisons rebellion and by crises at court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a>, a commander under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzhu-rong">Erzhu Rong</a>, turned on the Erzhu clan after Erzhu Rong's assassination and by 532 controlled the imperial government. Emperor Xiaowu sought support from the general Heba Yue; after Heba was assassinated, his army passed to his second in command, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, a descendant of the Xianbei Yuwen tribe.</p><p>In 534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and Xiaowu fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, so Gao made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaojing-of-eastern-wei">Yuan Shanjian</a> emperor, splitting the dynasty into Eastern Wei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>. The volcanic winter of 536 brought famine to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region. In early 537 Yuwen Tai defeated part of Gao Huan's invading army at the Battle of Xiaoguan, killing the general Dou Tai, and then seized the Hongnong Commandery to send its food west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> raised a new army, reportedly of up to 200,000 men, though some consider the figure too high, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>'s reported 10,000. He rejected the counsel of Xue Tang, who advised starving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, and of Hulü Qiangju, who proposed holding Yuwen Tai with one force while another took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. Yuwen Tai's general Li Bi chose the Weiqu marshland, ten miles from Shayuan, a reed-covered bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> that would funnel a large army inward.</p><p>Seeing the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> force before the reeds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> thought of burning them, but his generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> and Peng Le talked him out of it. As his troops advanced, Li Bi's cavalry burst from the reeds and cut the Eastern Wei army in two, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> attacked from the front. Panic spread and the whole army fled. Most of the losses came in the retreat: 6,000 Eastern Wei soldiers were killed and 70,000 captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Eastern Wei could no longer threaten the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> plain, and in 538 the Western general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugu-xin">Dugu Xin</a> briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. Western Wei was succeeded in 557 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>, who were in turn succeeded in 581 by the Sui Dynasty, which unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Shayuan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gao Huan balanced Xianbei soldiers and Chinese aristocrats and fought repeated wars with Western Wei, suffering defeat at <b>Shayuan</b> in 537 and failing to take Yubi in 546.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a></li></ul></section>
